<spell-notebook "theme-editor usage">
	<header level="1">
		Theme Editor
		<header>A theme editor makes a theme from an existing one:  its colours, fonts and shapes, live on real elements, then copied as a stylesheet or kept in the browser.</>
	</>
	<theme-editor-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#use-this-theme">Use this theme</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it, the kept theme, keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			A `<ui-theme-editor>` makes a theme by CHANGING one:  its base, `fruity` unless the page wears another.

			- The inspector, on the left:  the theme's name and base, then Color, Type and Shape.
			  Each choice starts as the base's own;  its Reset button (or Start over) puts it back.
			- The preview:  a sample app of real elements, once light and once dark.
			- The stylesheet:  every choice as tokens on `:root`, in `@layer ui.app`, so it wins over the base theme however each arrives.
			  Apply the base first, then this sheet:  that's the whole theme.
			- While it's on a page, the WHOLE page wears the theme it edits:
			  a token set on one element can't change what the page works out from it at `:root`.
			  Take it off the page and the page's own theme comes back.
			- `value` starts it from a theme you made before (`{"primary": "pink", "corners": "soft"}`);
			  `base` from another base.
			- Events:  `ui-changed` (`{ value, css }`) on every choice, `ui-copied` from Copy CSS,
			  `ui-saved` from Use this theme and Stop using.
			- Script:  `el.theme` (every choice now), `el.css` (the stylesheet).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			document.querySelector("ui-theme-editor").addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => {
			  console.log(event.detail.value.primary, event.detail.css)
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Use this theme">
		<markdown>
			Use this theme keeps the theme in this browser, and the Spell editor starts in it.
			Any page can wear it too:  `SavedTheme.restore()` applies its base, then its sheet.

			- `SavedTheme.has`, `.choices`, `.css`:  what's kept;  `SavedTheme.forget()` drops it.
			- Kept in `localStorage` (`spell-ui:theme`):  a private window keeps nothing.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			import { SavedTheme } from "spell-ui/components/ui-theme-editor"

			if (SavedTheme.has) await SavedTheme.restore()
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- Tab moves from field to field.
			- In a row of colours, one Tab stop:  the arrow keys move AND choose, as in any radio group.
			- The selects, the slider and the segmented choices take their own keys.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- Each field is labelled, with its tip behind its info icon.
			- A row of colours is a `radiogroup`, each swatch a `radio` named for its colour.
			- The sample app is real elements, so what it shows of contrast is what an app will have.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
